//! Baked binary Tang `ln(1 + i/M)` lookup table — the NARROW high
//! prefix (`M = 128`).
//!
//! GENERATED by `scripts/gen_ln_tang_table.py` (flint/Arb oracle —
//! every digit below is pinned by a rigorous interval bound). Do
//! NOT edit by hand; re-run the script and commit its output. This
//! file is NOT produced at build time — `build.rs` is untouched.
//!
//! Any change belongs in `scripts/gen_ln_tang_table.py`, never in
//! this file. Re-run the generator and commit both; a hand-edit here
//! is silently reverted the next time anyone regenerates.
//!
//! Each of the `M + 1 = 129` slots is the TRUNCATION of the matching
//! [`crate::algos::support::ln_tang_table`] slot to its top
//! `8` limbs. Both are emitted from the same oracle values in the
//! same generator run, so this table **is** the wide table's high-limb
//! prefix by construction — not a second data set to keep in step.
//! `ln_tang_slot`'s tests assert that identity wherever both compile.
//!
//! ## Why it exists
//!
//! Binary size, and nothing else. The wide table is `129 · 112 · 8 B`
//! ≈ 115 KB and is `_wide-support`-gated, so a narrow (`D18`/`D38`-only,
//! `no_std`) build must not carry it; this one is ≈ 8 KB.
//!
//! `B = 512` bits is sized against the narrow work rung `Int<12>`,
//! whose Ziv-escalation cap is `BITS/8 = 96` decimal digits — the
//! deepest working scale a narrow call can demand. The accessor needs
//! `ceil((96 · 3322/1000 + 64) / 64) = 6` limbs there, so the stored
//! `8` carry two limbs (≈ 38 decimal digits) of headroom.

